,8,8,8,5,8,4,9,9,9,9,9,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,9,9,9,9,9,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,7,5,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,5,7,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,7,5,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,5,7,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4],[4,2,5,2,2,2,2,2,2,5,2,4,7,5,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,5,7,4],[4,2,5,2,2,2,2,2,2,5,2,4,7,5,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,5,7,4],[4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4]],"output":[[9,9,9,9,9],[9,5,9,5,9],[9,5,9,5,9],[9,9,9,9,9],[9,9,9,9,9],[9,9,9,9,9],[9,5,9,5,9],[9,5,9,5,9],[9,9,9,9,9]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grid is made of.** Every input has a frame colour, the colour at the corner (4 in all pairs). It divides the grid into solid rectangles. Each rectangle has a fill colour. All rectangles but one also carry a small marker motif in a second colour (5, 7 or 6), placed symmetrically near their four corners. Exactly one rectangle has no markers. In pair 1 that is the 3‑rectangle, 5×7. In pair 2 it is the 8‑rectangle, 20×12. In pair 3 it is the 1‑rectangle, 10×6. The output is always that blank rectangle, at its own size, with the motif drawn into it.\n\n**How the motif transfers.** The marked rectangles have different sizes, so the motif cannot just be copied cell for cell. It stretches. In pair 1 the 5‑lines are 3 wide in a 5‑wide box, 4 wide in a 6‑wide box and 6 wide in an 8‑wide box. In every case they run from column 1 to column w−2.\n\nWhat stays fixed is each cell's distance to the nearest edge. Let dy = min(y, h−1−y) and dx = min(x, w−1−x).\n- Pair 1: every marker sits at dy=1, dx≥1. A 5×7 target therefore gets full lines on rows 1 and 3, which matches the output.\n- Pair 2: markers sit only at (dy,dx)=(1,1). A 20×12 target gets just the four inset corner dots, which matches.\n- Pair 3: markers sit at (1,1), (1,2) and (2,1). A 10×6 target gets rows \"6666\" and \"6..6\" at top and bottom, which matches.\n\n**Rule.**\n1. Collect every (dy,dx) pair that holds a marker in any marked rectangle.\n2. Fill the blank rectangle with its own colour.\n3. Paint the marker colour at every cell whose (dy,dx) is in the collected set.\n\nI checked these three examples against the outputs by reading the grids, not by running code.\n\n**Test.** The blank rectangle is the 9‑rectangle, 9 rows × 5 columns. The references put 5 at (dy,dx) = (1,1) and (2,1). So the output is a 9×5 field of 9 with 5s at columns 1 and 3, on rows 1, 2, 6 and 7.\n\n**Stages emitted.** First the blank canvas filled with its own colour, then the finished motif.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    sep = g[0, 0]\n    rects = components(g != sep)\n    infos = []\n    for cells in rects:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        sub = g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1]\n        vals, cnts = np.unique(sub, return_counts=True)\n        fill = vals[np.argmax(cnts)]\n        infos.append((sub, fill))\n    marked = set()\n    mcol = None\n    blank = None\n    for sub, fill in infos:\n        h, w = sub.shape\n        if np.all(sub == fill):\n            blank = (h, w, fill)\n            continue\n        for y in range(h):\n            for x in range(w):\n                if sub[y, x] != fill:\n                    mcol = int(sub[y, x])\n                    marked.add((min(y, h - 1 - y), min(x, w - 1 - x)))\n    h, w, fill = blank\n    out = np.full((h, w), fill, dtype=int)\n    emit(out, \"Allocate the marker-free rectangle as the canvas, filled with its own colour.\")\n    for y in range(h):\n        for x in range(w):\n            if (min(y, h - 1 - y), min(x, w - 1 - x)) in marked:\n                out[y, x] = mcol\n    emit(out, \"Paint markers wherever the edge-distance (dy,dx) matches a marker position in the reference rectangles.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"All rectangles but one also carry a small marker motif in a second colour (5, 7 or 6), placed symmetrically near their four corners."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"All rectangles but one also carry a small marker motif in a second colour (5, 7 or 6), placed symmetrically near their four corners."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The output is always that blank rectangle, at its own size, with the motif drawn into it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How the motif transfers.** The marked rectangles have different sizes, so the motif cannot just be copied cell for cell."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"What stays fixed is each cell's distance to the nearest edge."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"5c1c7be3b47716ea3621856683107412b743ba7e57666f1caf2ce4650415e4aa","task_sha256":"f7a26baaef4a6a2ec1d39d8c659af0f758c81e8942f96a99889b5850ad3ec09e","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
